const CartComponent = () => {
  const { items } = useSelector(state => state.cart);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  return (
    <div className="cart">
      <h2>Your Cart</h2>
      {items.length === 0 ? (
        <p>Your cart is empty</p>
      ) : (
        <ul>
          {items.map(item => (
            <li key={item.id} className="cart-item">
              <span>{item.name}</span>
              <span>{item.quantity}</span>
              <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))}>
                Remove
              </button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};
```

This uses the cart slice we defined earlier. Key things: `useSelector` to get the cart state, `useDispatch` to trigger the `removeItem` action, and the conditional rendering logic for empty state. The `key` prop on list items is critical for React's reconciliation. For production you'd want to add error boundaries and loading states too — but this is the core pattern.

