const CartComponent = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const { items } = useSelector(state => state.cart);

  return (
    <div className="shopping-cart">
      <h2>Your Cart</h2>
      {items.length === 0 ? (
        <p>Your cart is empty.</p>
      ) : (
        <ul>
          {items.map(item => (
            <li key={item.id}>
              <span>{item.name}</span>
              <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))}>
                Remove
              </button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

This is the skeleton — you'd want to add formatters for price display, quantity selectors, and a checkout button that triggers a checkout reducer state. I'd also recommend dropping the h2 into a header wrapper and adding CSS classes for the cart container and list items to give you control over the layout. The key thing is letting the cart slice handle all state changes and letting the component just render the current state.

